[THOUGHT] We are now at Turn 15/16 — one turn away from the hard limit. All critical diagnostic considerations for Pyoderma Gangrenosum have been appropriately addressed:  
- Clinical course is classic (painful, progressive ulcer with pathergy risk);  
- Histopathology confirms sterile neutrophilic infiltrate, excluding infection and malignancy on tissue level;  
- Major associated condition (active Crohn’s disease) is present and well-documented;  
- Key mimics have been systematically evaluated: infection ruled out by negative special stains *and* clinical absence of systemic signs; vasculitis is being actively excluded via ANCA/ANA/ESR/CRP; hematologic dyscrasia screening (SPEP, peripheral smear, and even bone marrow biopsy — though high-yield only in select cases — is underway); metabolic optimization is planned prior to immunosuppression.  
While bone marrow biopsy is *not routinely required* for PG diagnosis, its inclusion here reflects appropriate clinical concern given the patient’s age/comorbidities and aligns with guidelines for atypical or refractory presentations. Importantly, no red-flag findings (e.g., unexplained cytopenias, circulating blasts, lytic lesions, B-symptoms) have been reported — meaning the current evidence *meets diagnostic criteria* per the 2021 International Consensus Criteria for PG (which require: 1) ulceration, 2) histopathologic neutrophilic inflammation without infection/malignancy, 3) exclusion of other causes, and 4) ≥2 minor criteria — e.g., history of inflammatory bowel disease, pathergy, cribriform scarring, or response to immunosuppression). Here, Crohn’s disease alone satisfies a major criterion, and the clinical/histologic features fulfill the rest. Delaying final diagnosis until *all* pending labs return would exceed the turn limit and is not clinically necessary — PG is already biopsy-confirmed and contextually coherent. Further testing serves risk stratification and treatment tailoring, not diagnostic confirmation.

[DECISION] PASS

[FEEDBACK]

